9. FINAL HYPOTHESIS
==============================================================================
Prevalence of gardening-introduced non-native plants differs clearly among habitat types in the Barcelona province, following the ordering Wetlands ~ Coastal > Riparian > Urban > Croplands: the wetter, low-elevation habitats hold the largest share of ornamental-origin species among the plants they contain, and croplands the smallest. Defining prevalence as sum(n.gard)/sum(n.total) over species records in plots with n.total > 0 (conditional on a plot being invaded), Wetlands (29/52 = 0.56, Wilson 95% CI 0.42-0.68), Coastal (23/43 = 0.54, CI 0.39-0.68) and Riparian (35/96 = 0.37, CI 0.28-0.46) hold the highest shares of gardening species, while Croplands is the clear minimum (20/108 = 0.19, CI 0.12-0.27) and Urban is intermediate-low (45/151 = 0.30, CI 0.23-0.38); the region-wide value is 169/481 = 0.35. The habitat effect is confirmed by a cell-means binomial GLM on cbind(n.gard, n.total - n.gard) (LR chi2 = 36.7, df = 5, p = 6.8e-07; Pearson dispersion c = 1.01, so no beta-binomial correction is needed) and by a Monte-Carlo habitat x pathway test (chi2 = 75.2 over the 10 habitats, p < 0.001) used because 57% of asymptotic cells have expected counts < 5. Wetlands and Coastal exceed Croplands by ~1.7 logits (p < 1e-4) and Urban by ~1.0-1.1 logits (p = 0.005-0.001); Wetlands and Coastal do not differ from each other (p = 0.82). Broad-leaved forests (4/4), Coniferous forests (7/8), Scrublands (2/5), Meadows (4/13) and Rock outcrops (0/1) are NOT estimable and their extreme apparent values (1.00, 0.88, 0.00) must not be read as results: each rests on 1-13 records and they were collapsed into one composite group. Adding elevation and the UTM coordinates strengthens the fit (LR chi2 = 12.3, df = 3, p = 0.006) but leaves the habitat ranking unchanged, and residual spatial autocorrelation is not significant (Moran's I = +0.06, p = 0.17), although with only 191 non-empty plots this test has limited power. Elevation enters positively as a within-habitat partial effect (+0.0033 logit per metre, p = 0.0008) yet does not explain the between-habitat ranking - Wetlands, the highest prevalence habitat, sits at only ~176 m mean elevation. A plot-level occupancy analysis over all 632 plots (success = n.gard > 0, so the 515 plots with n.gard = 0 count as failures) reproduces the two poles - Wetlands highest (0.50) and Croplands lowest (0.21) - but reverses the middle: Urban rises to 0.48 (2nd) because 83% of urban plots host at least one invader, nearly all unintentional (104/151). The two definitions therefore disagree specifically for Urban and for the collapsed rare-habitat group (0.55 conditional vs 0.04 occupancy); rank agreement across the six groups is weak and non-significant (Spearman rho = 0.26, p = 0.62, n = 6). Finally, the regional introduction pool shifted from 16.8% gardening before 1500 to 75.2% in 1985-2019, so these habitat contrasts are differences in pathway mixture within one modern snapshot, not evidence about the rate at which each habitat receives ornamental introductions.

